description:
memory.rs:737-749 (create_object), 830-842 (update_object), 876-889 (destroy_object) — three call sites construct the same ChangeRecord shape from a serde_json::Value:

    let record = ChangeRecord {
        id: <id-of-this-mutation>,
        related_type: val.get("relatedType").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or("").to_owned(),
        type_name: val.get("@type").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or("").to_owned(),
    };

The three blocks are byte-identical modulo the id-source. Idiomatic Rust extracts a small constructor or impl block:

    impl ChangeRecord {
        fn from_stored(id: Id, val: &serde_json::Value) -> Self {
            Self {
                id,
                related_type: val.get("relatedType").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or("").to_owned(),
                type_name: val.get("@type").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or("").to_owned(),
            }
        }
    }

— callers become `ChangeRecord::from_stored(server_id.clone(), &val)` etc.

Why this matters beyond aesthetics: the load-bearing invariant documented in JMAP-ayoz.19 (capture-at-mutation pattern for destroyed-array §3.3 filter conformance) is currently expressed by repetition. A future contributor 'simplifying' one of the three sites and not the others would silently break the invariant. A single constructor with rustdoc citing JMAP-ayoz.19 makes the contract local instead of triplicated.

This is a P3 idiom finding (not P2) because the three sites currently DO produce the correct result — the bug is latent in future maintenance, not present today. JMAP-ayoz.31 already extracted DRY for the change-log overflow check; the same pattern applies here.

Cross-references:
- JMAP-ayoz.11 (sentinel-string smell on .related_type / .type_name)
- JMAP-ayoz.19 (capture-at-mutation is load-bearing for §3.3 destroyed-array)
- JMAP-ayoz.31 (precedent: max_changes overflow check DRY refactor)
